      <h2 id="faq">What Is a Doula? FAQ</h2>
      <div class="not-prose space-y-3 mb-8">
        <details open class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">What is a doula?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">A doula is a trained birth professional who provides emotional, physical, and informational support during pregnancy, labor, and postpartum. Doulas do not perform medical tasks. They provide continuous support through labor, helping with comfort measures, breathing, positions, and advocacy for your birth plan.</div>
        </details>
        <details open class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">What doula services are included?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">A standard birth doula package includes 1 to 2 prenatal visits to build your birth plan, on-call availability from around 38 weeks, continuous labor support from active labor through delivery, immediate postpartum help with feeding and skin-to-skin, and 1 to 2 postpartum check-in visits. Some doulas also offer postpartum doula services as a separate hourly package for newborn care, breastfeeding support, and emotional recovery.</div>
        </details>
        <details open class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">What does a doula do?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">During labor, a doula helps you find positions that ease pain, reminds you to breathe, gives your partner suggestions for how to help, explains what is happening in plain language, speaks up for your preferences, and steps in if your partner needs to step out. Before birth, doulas help you build your birth plan. After birth, they check in on healing, feeding, and emotional recovery.</div>
        </details>
        <details open class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">What is the difference between a doula and a midwife?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">A midwife is a medical professional who provides prenatal care, delivers your baby, and manages clinical aspects of birth. A doula is a non-medical support person who provides continuous support through labor, focusing on comfort, emotional support, and advocacy. A midwife handles the medical side. A doula handles the emotional and physical side. Many families hire both.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">How much does a doula cost?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">In most US cities, birth doulas charge between $800 and $2,500 for a full package including prenatal visits, on-call availability, continuous labor support, and postpartum visits. The national average is around $1,200 to $1,500. Some doulas offer sliding-scale fees, and Medicaid covers doulas in more than a dozen states.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">Does a doula replace my partner?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">No. A doula complements your partner, not replaces them. A doula knows birth and can guide your partner on how to help with counter-pressure, breathing, and positioning. When your partner needs a break, your doula is there. Many partners say having a doula made them feel more confident and useful, not less involved.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">Can a doula help if I am having an induction or C-section?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">Yes. Doulas are especially valuable during inductions and cesareans, where Pitocin, continuous monitoring, and more interventions are standard. A doula helps you adapt your birth plan for the experience you are actually having, advocates for your preferences, and provides continuous support through a longer or more complex labor.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">How do I find a doula near me?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">Start your search 3 to 4 months before your due date. Look in doula directories like DONA International, local Facebook birth groups, ask your childbirth class instructor or care provider for referrals, or check with local birth centers. You can also browse our city birth support guides for local resources.</div>
        </details>
        <details class="bg-tjb-cream-50 rounded-lg border border-tjb-lavender-200/50 group">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer p-4 font-semibold text-tjb-charcoal">Are doulas evidence-based?</summary>
          <div class="px-4 pb-4 text-tjb-gray leading-relaxed">Yes. A landmark Cochrane review of over 15,000 women found that continuous labor support from a doula leads to 28% fewer C-sections, 31% less Pitocin use, 9% less pain medication, shorter labors by an average of 40 minutes, and higher satisfaction with the birth experience overall.</div>
        </details>
      </div>

